ise-en-dev","id":123107610,"node_id":"U_kgDOB1Z5Gg","gravatar_id":"","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"content_type":"text/plain; charset=utf-8","state":"uploaded","size":7057,"digest":"sha256:c623e902d29b8fa18343e161d7c3f3bc46d05f7da40900ba61a21f33f13525af","download_count":13,"created_at":"2026-11-18T14:55:24Z","updated_at":"2026-11-18T14:55:25Z"},{"id":763801879,"node_id":"RA_kwDOIvux3s4thrEX","name":"SHASUMS512.txt","label":"","uploader":{"login":"mise-en-dev","id":123107610,"node_id":"U_kgDOB1Z5Gg","gravatar_id":"","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"content_type":"text/plain; charset=utf-8","state":"uploaded","size":6175,"digest":"sha256:9503c444e40ce211f3c4a274e1a739096f9997c601c1d4870a755690399324c6","download_count":62,"created_at":"2026-11-18T14:55:24Z","updated_at":"2026-11-18T14:55:25Z"},{"id":763801906,"node_id":"RA_kwDOIvux3s4thrEy","name":"SHASUMS512.txt.minisig","label":"","uploader":{"login":"mise-en-dev","id":123107610,"node_id":"U_kgDOB1Z5Gg","gravatar_id":"","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"content_type":"application/octet-stream","state":"uploaded","size":309,"digest":"sha256:59b971e33f6e3ef087549ffd92d7aedee45bfceb4dab469296712536050996ab","download_count":61,"created_at":"2026-11-18T14:55:25Z","updated_at":"2026-11-18T14:55:25Z"},{"id":763803488,"node_id":"RA_kwDOIvux3s4thrdg","name":"v2026.11.10.tar.gz.sig","label":"","uploader":{"login":"mise-en-dev","id":123107610,"node_id":"U_kgDOB1Z5Gg","gravatar_id":"","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"content_type":"application/pgp-signature","state":"uploaded","size":566,"digest":"sha256:229a82de90e0e4dd9464285a383800292aec091f62bde1fa71ffa83e6e2732ca","download_count":15,"created_at":"2026-11-18T14:55:54Z","updated_at":"2026-11-18T14:55:54Z"}],"body":"`mise lock` gains an offline `--check` mode for CI, and `--dry-run` now shows what would actually change. New options let you limit mise to an allowlist of backends, choose musl or glibc builds for a single tool, pin PyPI packages to private uv indexes, and exclude git-ignored files from task `sources`. This release also fixes problems with lockfiles, Java build ordering, `mise exec` PATH order, Homebrew formulas, and shims.\n\n## Added\n\n### Lockfiles\n\n- **`mise lock --check`.** Checks that `mise.lock` matches `mise.toml` without writing anything or using the network. It fails with a list of everything `mise install --locked` would reject: a version that isn't locked (for example, when an update bot bumps `mise.toml` but not the lockfile), a platform entry with no URL, or a checksum that is missing or malformed. It also reports missing GitHub additional artifacts and missing or edited npm/PyPI dependency graphs. Platforms are chosen the same way `mise lock` chooses them. `--json` prints one object per platform entry. It can't be combined with `--bump`, `--upgrade`, `--dry-run`, `--minimum-release-age`, `--sidecars` or a `tool@version` argument. [#15768](https://github.com/jdx/mise/pull/15768) (@jdx, @jmoreno11)\n\n  ```console\n  $ mise lock --check\n  mise ERROR ~/src/app/mise.lock would fail a locked install:\n    - jq@1.6 is not in the lockfile\n    - No lockfile checksum found for yq@4.44.1 on platform linux-x64 (...) (--locked mode)\n  hint: run `mise lock` to update the lockfile\n  ```\n\n- **Provenance in `--check` output.** Each platform entry now shows what it was verified with when it was locked: `minisign`, `cosign`, `slsa`, `github-attestations`, `checksum only`, or `none`. `--json` adds a matching `provenance` field. This information is for display only and doesn't change whether the check passes. [#15769](https://github.com/jdx/mise/pull/15769) (@jdx, @jmoreno11)\n\n### Backends\n\n- **`enable_backends` allowlist.** Only the backends you list can resolve or install tools. Registry shorthands fall back to their next allowed backend, and an identifier that uses a backend you didn't list fails with an error that names the setting. `disable_backends` still applies on top of the allowlist. An empty list, the default, allows every backend. The setting checks each tool's configured backend, not tools that a backend runs itself (such as the `node` used by `npm:`). `mise install-into` now respects both settings. [#15766](https://github.com/jdx/mise/pull/15766) (@jdx, @jmoreno11)\n\n  ```toml\n  [settings]\n  enable_backends = [\"core\", \"aqua\", \"github\"]\n  ```\n\n- **Per-tool `libc` for packslip and github tools.** On Linux, `libc = \"musl\"` or `libc = \"glibc\"` (also `gnu`) selects the build for one tool without changing the global `libc` setting. Once you set it, mise never falls back to the other libc. It errors if no matching build exists. The option is recorded in the lockfile, and you need `mise install --force` to replace an existing install after changing it. It also works with the gitlab and forgejo backends. [#15765](https://github.com/jdx/mise/pull/15765) (@jdx, @jmoreno11)\n\n  ```toml\n  [tools]\n  fnox = { version = \"latest\", libc = \"musl\" }\n  ```\n\n- **`filter_bins` for `http:` tools.** Only the named executables are put on `PATH`, instead of the whole `bin_path` directory. The `cursor-agent` registry entry now uses this, so its bundled `node` and `rg` no longer override your own. [#15835](https://github.com/jdx/mise/pull/15835) (@jdx)\n\n- **Private uv indexes for `pypi:` packages.** The new `indexes` and `sources` tool options take individual packages (the tool or its `with`/`expose` requirements) from a named index, while everything else stays on the default index. They mirror uv's `tool.uv.index` and `tool.uv.sources`, require uv and a dependency graph, and are recorded in `mise.lock`. Index URLs can't contain credentials. Use `UV_INDEX_<NAME>_USERNAME` and `UV_INDEX_<NAME>_PASSWORD` instead. [#15840](https://github.com/jdx/mise/pull/15840) (@jdx, @sisp)\n\n  ```toml\n  [tools.\"pypi:zensical\"]\n  version = \"0.1.1\"\n  with = [\"my-zensical-theme==1.2.3\"]\n  indexes = [{ name = \"private\", url = \"<your private index URL>\", explicit = true }]\n  sources = { my-zensical-theme = { index = \"private\" } }\n  ```\n\n### Tasks\n\n- **`!@gitignore` in `sources`.** This entry removes git-ignored, untracked files from what the earlier entries matched, so build output or `node_modules` no longer makes a task stale. Like a `!` pattern, it is evaluated in order, so a later entry can add an ignored path back. Tracked files always count as sources. Only the repository's own `.gitignore` files and `.git/info/exclude` are used, not your global excludes file. [#15796](https://github.com/jdx/mise/pull/15796) (@jdx, @brodienguyen)\n\n  ```toml\n  [tasks.build]\n  sources = [\"pkg/**\", \"!@gitignore\", \"pkg/dist/**\"]\n  ```\n\n- **Task completion offers one spelling per task.** In a monorepo, `mise run <TAB>` now suggests only the shortest name for each task. Typing `:` shows names qualified for the current config root, and `//` shows absolute paths. Every spelling still runs. [#15832](https://github.com/jdx/mise/pull/15832) (@jdx)\n\n- **Set one lock pool at a time.** `mise settings set task.lock_pools.cpu 8` and `mise settings unset task.lock_pools.cpu` change a single entry without replacing the whole map. This works for any map setting, such as `url_replacements`. Pool sizes can also come from `MISE_TASK_LOCK_POOLS`, as `cpu=8,gpu=1` or a JSON object. [#15794](https://github.com/jdx/mise/pull/15794) (@jdx)\n\n### Bootstrap\n\n- **Enable services without starting them.** In `[bootstrap.services]`, `state = \"unchanged\"` only manages whether a unit starts at boot or login. mise never starts, stops, reloads or restarts it. This is useful for units such as a login manager. It works for system units and existing user units. It is rejected with `masked = true` and for user services that mise defines with `command` or `builtin`. [#15759](https://github.com/jdx/mise/pull/15759) (@jdx, @Vantesh)\n\n  ```toml\n  [bootstrap.services.greetd]\n  state = \"unchanged\"\n  enabled = true\n  ```\n\n- `[bootstrap.files]` and `[bootstrap.directories]` now accept `permissions` as another name for `mode`, the same key `[dotfiles]` uses. Setting both is an error. [#15849](https://github.com/jdx/mise/pull/15849) (@jdx, @syhol)\n\n### Registry\n\n- Added `cargo-nextest`. [#15854](https://github.com/jdx/mise/pull/15854) (@jdx, @FalkWoldmann)\n\n## Changed\n\n- **`mise lock --dry-run` reports real changes.** It now resolves every tool and platform the same way `mise lock` does and compares the result with the lockfile on disk. Each entry is listed as a new entry, a version change, a new platform, changed fields (such as `checksum filled`), a removal, or `unchanged`. Because of this, a dry run now makes network requests, may download artifacts to compute checksums, and fails wherever `mise lock` would fail. `--json` output is unchanged. [#15767](https://github.com/jdx/mise/pull/15767) (@jdx, @jmoreno11)\n- **`tool_config.locked` lockfiles are left alone after installs.** When every config that writes a lockfile sets `[tool_config] locked = true`, `mise install`, `mise exec`, `mise use` and other install paths no longer add platforms, refresh entries or prune stale tools in that lockfile. Only `mise lock` (and `mise upgrade` for newer versions) changes it. As a result, entries for removed tools now stay until you run `mise lock`. [#15761](https://github.com/jdx/mise/pull/15761) (@jdx, @jmoreno11)\n- The packages a plugin needs from the system (its `systemDependencies`) are now installed with the Linux distribution's package manager (`apk`, `apt`, `dnf`, `pacman`, `zypper`) when it has a hint for them, instead of mise's built-in Homebrew. Libraries installed through Homebrew could not be found by the system `pkg-config` or compiler. [#15839](https://github.com/jdx/mise/pull/15839) (@jdx)\n\n## Fixed\n\n- Java: with several builds of the same version installed (such as Temurin `+7` and `+101`), `mise ls`, the runtime symlinks (`latest`, `temurin-25`, ...), lockfile selection and `mise outdated` now treat the higher build number as newest. [#15756](https://github.com/jdx/mise/pull/15756) (@jdx)\n- When several `mise install` processes ran at once, an `npm:` tool could fail because its `node@lts` dependency was \"not installed\", even after another process had just installed it. [#15757](https://github.com/jdx/mise/pull/15757) (@jdx)\n- `mise install <tool>` and `mise x <tool>` now fail with a clear message when the tool's `os` option excludes the current platform. Previously the error was about a missing release asset. [#15826](https://github.com/jdx/mise/pull/15826) (@jdx)\n- With `tool_config.locked`, a tool limited to another OS no longer prints a \"not in the lockfile\" warning on every command. [#15848](https://github.com/jdx/mise/pull/15848) (@jdx)\n- When `mise lock` refuses an aqua SLSA provenance downgrade, the error now suggests setting `slsa_signer_identity` and `slsa_signer_issuer` in the tool options. [#15827](https://github.com/jdx/mise/pull/15827) (@jdx)\n- `mise x -- <cmd>` runs the pinned tool's binary again, for example the pinned npm instead of Node's bundled npm, even when the inherited PATH already contains that tool's bin directory. [#15838](https://github.com/jdx/mise/pull/15838) (@jdx)\n- Inactive shims that fall back to a system binary no longer run `rustup` checks when `rust` is configured. These checks could interfere with `mise up`. [#15795](https://github.com/jdx/mise/pull/15795) (@jdx)\n- `npm:` installs with provenance verification work again on images without system CA certificates (such as `node:24-slim`). The sigstore TUF fetch now uses mise's HTTP client, which falls back to the bundled roots. [#15829](https://github.com/jdx/mise/pull/15829) (@jdx)\n- Homebrew formulas: source builds from third-party taps that use `HOMEBREW_PREFIX` and related constants now work. Files that a formula installs into `bin` or `sbin` are now executable. [#15834](https://github.com/jdx/mise/pull/15834), [#15836](https://github.com/jdx/mise/pull/15836) (@jdx)\n- Ruby previews such as `ruby = \"4.1.0-preview1\"` now install from precompiled builds that are published as GitHub pre-releases, instead of failing or compiling from source. [#15830](https://github.com/jdx/mise/pull/15830) (@jdx)\n- Installing `rust@beta` or `rust@nightly` with a `minimum_release_age` cutoff now stops after `fetch_remote_versions_timeout` with a clear error, instead of searching a slow dist server for minutes. [#15847](https://github.com/jdx/mise/pull/15847) (@jdx)\n- `mise prune` no longer marks the commit that `latest` currently points to as prunable for an `npm:` git source that tracks the default branch. If mise can't reach the remote, it keeps every install. [#15837](https://github.com/jdx/mise/pull/15837) (@jdx)\n- `mise ls --all-sources` no longer hides a tool when a different tool is pinned to the same version. [#15833](https://github.com/jdx/mise/pull/15833) (@jdx)\n- `mise bootstrap --from <url> --update` now pulls the source repository before loading its config. A source whose current commit has a broken config can now pick up its own fix. [#15841](https://github.com/jdx/mise/pull/15841) (@jdx, @Guria)\n\n## Documentation\n\n- The task docs now say that a dependency requested with different arguments runs once for each set of arguments. [#15855](https://github.com/jdx/mise/pull/15855) (@jdx)\n\n**Full Changelog**: https://github.com/jdx/mise/compare/v2026.11.9...v2026.11.10\n\n## 💚 Sponsor mise\n\nmise is built and maintained by [@jdx](https://github.com/jdx), an open source developer at [**entire.io**](https://entire.io/), the title sponsor of his open source work.\n\nIf mise saves you or your team time, please consider becoming an [individual or company sponsor](https://jdx.dev/sponsors.html). Your support funds ongoing development and helps keep mise fast, free, and independent.\n","reactions":{"total_count":10,"+1":3,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":2,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":5,"eyes":0},"mentions_count":8}
